WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.671
A certificate can be valid
and unrevoked and still fail,

2
00:00:03.671 --> 00:00:08.315
because every check that makes it
trustworthy starts with a DNS lookup.

3
00:00:08.315 --> 00:00:13.067
If the revocation names live only in
Active Directory DNS, a cloud workload

4
00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:17.430
can't reach them, and the ticket gets
opened as a certificate problem.

5
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:21.010
The same goes for sign-in
endpoints and wildcard names.

6
00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.505
DNSSEC protects the answers themselves.

7
00:00:23.505 --> 00:00:27.012
The program's position: sign
your external zones always,

8
00:00:27.012 --> 00:00:32.271
validate on resolvers you control, and sign
internal zones only where it's required.

9
00:00:32.271 --> 00:00:33.733
The CISO sets that.

10
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:37.507
And moving a zone somewhere it can't
be signed is a risk acceptance,

11
00:00:37.507 --> 00:00:39.810
so the authorizing official decides it.

12
00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.280
Next, addresses.
